  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES imagery captures the remnants of two convective \nclusters that developed across ND/northern MN and NE/IA overnight.\nOur forecast area remained dry between these clusters, though we\nhave observed expansive mid-to-high clouds associated with \nconvective debris. Visible satellite loop displays clouds \nthinning in the wake of the morning convection, which should \nallow for some more sun to filter through this afternoon and \nsupport forecast highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s. \n\nLooking upstream, a closed upper-low is captured on water vapor \nimagery over central Saskatchewan. The feature is forecast to move \neastward across Manitoba tonight and then pivot southeastward \ntowards Lake Superior heading into Wednesday. Associated surface low \npressure will follow a similar track, which will send a cold front \nfrom west to east across the Upper Midwest. Winds have turned \nsoutherly ahead of the incoming system, which will support both \ntemperature and moisture advection. Sensibly, it will be a mild \nnight with lows in the mid 60s across most of the forecast area \n(slightly cooler into western WI) and warmer temperatures (mid \n80s) follow for Tuesday's highs. In addition, dew points will be\non the rise over the next 24-hours, which will yield sufficient\ninstability for thunderstorms ahead of the front. We're looking\nat two potential rounds of convection tied to the frontal \npassage: 1) Late tonight into Tuesday morning, mainly across \nwestern and central MN. 2) Tuesday afternoon and evening, \ngenerally developing across far eastern MN (I-35) and moving \neast into western WI.\n\n1) Late Tonight - Tuesday Morning...Thunderstorms are forecast to \ndevelop across the Dakotas this afternoon and progress eastward into \nnorthwestern MN tonight. The storms are forecast to outrun a plume \nof stronger instability across the Dakotas, which will result in \na weakening trend by the time they advance into northwestern \nportions of the MPX forecast area. Forecast soundings at AXN and\nSTC highlight this idea, with skinny elevated instability \nlocated above very stable low-levels. SPC has maintained a \nSlight Risk across portions of eastern ND/SD and far NW MN, \nwhere a more favorable environment for deeper convection \nexists. Locally, communities across far western MN are under a \nMarginal Risk (1/5) for the potential for an isolated instance \nof damaging wind or hail as the storms approach after 10 PM. \nThis potential quickly drops-off as storms move east across \ncentral MN after midnight.\n\n2) Tuesday Afternoon/Evening...Weakening showers and isolated \nthunderstorms will likely be ongoing Tuesday morning across portions \nof the area. While there is a level of uncertainty regarding \natmospheric recovery following the morning precipitation/clouds, \nongoing southwesterly flow ahead of the true surface front should \naid in destabilization and yield a narrow plume of CAPEs on the \norder of ~1000-2000 J/kg. In addition to the instability, a \njetstreak south of the upper-low will support 30-35kts of effective\nshear, supportive of deeper convection. One of the forecast \nchallenges over the past few days has narrowing down areal and \ntemporal uncertainties with regards to convective initiation. \nWhile a few \"western\" solutions suggest afternoon CI across \nportions of central to southern MN, the latest HREF/REFS \nensemble consensus follows trends in ML guidance that favors CI \nacross eastern MN. With this in mind, we'll look for locations \nalong and east of I-35 as having the best chance for strong to \nsevere convection Tuesday afternoon and evening. Deeper analysis\nshows an environment favorable for a few isolated supercell \nstructures across western WI, where elongated low-level \ncurvature is present in hodographs between RNH/EAU. SPC has \nmaintained a Marginal Risk (1/5) across much of the forecast \narea, which is reasonable given the overall modest convective \nsetup with uncertainties regarding destabilization. Should \nsurface based convection realize the potential environment, \ndamaging winds, large hail, and a tornado or two would be \npossible. \n\nConvection exits the forecast area by 10 PM Tuesday evening and\nwill be followed by a mostly quiet second half of the work \nweek. The core of the upper-low will continue to dig \nsoutheastward into the Great Lakes Wednesday, which in turn will\nsupport breezy northwesterly flow across the Upper Midwest. \nWe'll run with a dry forecast for now, however we eventually may\nneed to include isolated to scattered shower chances within the\nmid-level CAA regime Wednesday afternoon. Surface high pressure\nbuilds east Thursday and Friday and will bring a pleasant end \nto the work week with highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s along \nwith comfortable dew points. The extended forecast features the \nreturn of a more active pattern as a series of Pacific shortwaves\ntraverse the northern periphery of the Four Corners ridge that \nhas dominated the weather",
